        {"t": "h2", "text": "Concept block"},
        {"t": "b", "items": [
            "A class is a reference type. A struct is a value type and lives inline in its container. Copying a large struct copies the data. Boxing wraps a value type in an object and allocates.",
            "A record is a class with value-based equality, unless it is a record struct. Use a record for a DTO or a message. Use a class when identity matters, such as an entity tracked by EF Core.",
            "ref passes a variable by reference and the caller must assign it first. out passes by reference and the callee must assign it. in passes a readonly reference and avoids copying a large readonly struct.",
            "string is immutable. Concatenation in a loop allocates. StringBuilder, or a string.Create / interpolation in modern code for a single result, is the conscious choice. An interpolated string still allocates the result.",
            "LINQ to Objects is deferred. The query runs when you iterate. A second foreach runs it again. IQueryable carries expression trees so EF Core can become SQL. Materialize with ToListAsync at the boundary.",
            "async methods return Task or ValueTask and release the thread during the await. async void is for event handlers only. A library method takes a CancellationToken and passes it down.",
            "ASP.NET Core does not capture a SynchronizationContext. Blocking on .Result is still a thread-pool stall and a deadlock risk in older ASP.NET or UI contexts. In a Web API it shows up as thread starvation under load.",
            "Equals and GetHashCode stay in sync. A record gives you that. A mutable object used as a dictionary key changes buckets when mutated.",
            "IDisposable and IAsyncDisposable release unmanaged resources and owned disposables. using and await using define the lifetime. A DI container disposes what it created, for the matching lifetime.",
            "Nullable reference types are a compile-time contract. Enable them on new projects. A lead treats a warning as a review comment, with a suppression only next to a proven invariant.",
            "lock uses a private reference object. Do not lock on this, a Type, or a string. ConcurrentDictionary, Channel, and SemaphoreSlim cover the cases lock makes awkward. async code waits with SemaphoreSlim.WaitAsync, because lock cannot cross an await.",
            "C# features worth naming from the last few versions: records, required members, primary constructors, collection expressions, the field keyword in C# 14 property accessors, and extension blocks for extension members. Name one you use. Skip the feature tour.",
        ]},
        {"t": "qa", "q": "What is the difference between a class, a struct, and a record, and when do you pick each?",
         "a": "A class has reference identity and lives on the heap. I use it for domain entities, services, and anything EF Core tracks. A struct is a value, copied on assignment, and I keep it small and immutable: a custom id wrapper, a coordinate, a range. A large mutable struct gets copied by mistake and those copies diverge. A record is usually a reference type with value equality, a compact constructor, and a useful ToString. I use records for API contracts, messages, and internal results. A record struct is the value-type version when the data is small and equality should compare contents. EF entities stay classes, because the change tracker depends on reference identity.",
         "lead": "In review I reject a 200-byte struct that is passed through the API layer, and I reject a record used as an EF entity unless the team has a measured reason. The default is class for identity, record for data that is defined by its values."},
        {"t": "qa", "q": "Explain ref, out, in, and params without reading a definition.",
         "a": "ref means the method reads and writes the caller's variable, and the caller initializes it. out means the method must assign the variable before returning; TryParse is the pattern. in means the method receives a readonly reference, which matters for large readonly structs so the call does not copy them. params packs arguments into an array, and in current runtimes params collections can avoid an array in some cases. I almost never design a public API with ref or out. I return a result object or a tuple. Try-pattern methods are the exception the BCL already taught every C# developer.",
         "lead": "A public ref parameter in application code is a design smell in a Web API. It makes ownership unclear and it does not cross an HTTP boundary. I ask the author to return a value."},
        {"t": "qa", "q": "Why does this LINQ query hit the database twice, and what is IEnumerable versus IQueryable?",
         "a": "IQueryable holds an expression tree. EF Core translates it when you enumerate, usually at ToListAsync, CountAsync, or a foreach. If I build IQueryable<Order> query = db.Orders.Where(...) and then both CountAsync and ToListAsync, that is two SQL statements, which is often what I want for a page plus a total. If I assign it to IEnumerable too early, further Where clauses run in memory after the SQL has already returned too many rows. Deferred execution also means a query defined once and enumerated twice runs twice. The boundary rule is: keep IQueryable inside the data method, apply filters, then materialize. Do not return IQueryable from a service that the controller will keep composing, because the SQL shape becomes everybody's problem and the DbContext lifetime gets tangled.",
         "lead": "I want paging, filtering, and the SQL projection in one place. Returning IQueryable from a repository looks flexible and makes query cost invisible. I return a finished list or a paged result."},
        {"t": "qa", "q": "Walk through async and await, and tell me where production systems get it wrong.",
         "a": "An async method returns a Task that represents the operation. At an await of an incomplete task, the method yields. The continuation runs later, normally on a thread-pool thread in ASP.NET Core. The request thread is free to serve other work. The bug I look for is sync-over-async: calling .Result or .Wait on an ASP.NET request. That blocks a thread-pool thread while the operation needs a thread to finish, and under load the pool starves. The second bug is async void outside an event handler, because exceptions skip your try/catch and the caller cannot wait. The third is a missing CancellationToken. When the client disconnects, the handler should stop the EF command and the HTTP call. I pass the token from the controller into the handler, into EF, and into HttpClient. I use Task for public async methods. ValueTask is for a hot path that often completes synchronously, and I do not await a ValueTask more than once.",
         "lead": "A lead standard: every IO method takes a CancellationToken, controllers pass HttpContext.RequestAborted, and a blocked .Result fails review. I also cap outbound parallelism. Starting 500 tasks with Task.WhenAll against one SQL database is a self-inflicted outage."},
        {"t": "qa", "q": "How do equality and hash codes work, and why do records change the conversation?",
         "a": "ReferenceEquals asks if two variables point at the same object. Equals, by default on a class, does the same. Value types compare contents. GetHashCode must match Equals: equal objects must share a hash code, and a hash code must stay stable while the object is in a dictionary or hash set. If I override one, I override the other, and I implement IEquatable<T> on value-like types to avoid boxing. A record synthesizes equality and hash code from its members. That is what I want for a message or a DTO. It is the wrong default for an entity whose identity is the primary key and whose other fields change. Anonymous types and tuples also use value equality, which is useful in tests.",
         "lead": "I ban mutable dictionary keys. I have seen a cache miss that was actually a mutated property inside a key object. Records for contracts, classes with key equality for entities, and a test that locks the contract."},
        {"t": "qa", "q": "How should a service dispose resources in ASP.NET Core?",
         "a": "If a type owns a resource, it implements IDisposable or IAsyncDisposable and releases that resource in Dispose or DisposeAsync. A using declaration keeps the lifetime on the screen. For async cleanup I use await using. In DI, the container disposes the instances it created: at the end of the request for scoped services, and at host shutdown for singletons. DbContext is scoped and disposed by the scope. I do not dispose a singleton inside a request. I do not dispose an instance I received if I do not own it; the factory or the caller does. HttpClient created through IHttpClientFactory is a special case: the factory owns the handlers, and disposing the client per call is safe because the handler pool stays.",
         "lead": "The review comment I write most often is ownership. A new SqlConnection inside a method needs a using. A connection injected from a pool is not disposed by business code. Unclear ownership becomes a leak that shows up only under load."},
        {"t": "qa", "q": "What is boxing, and when does it matter on a Web API?",
         "a": "Boxing allocates an object to hold a value type when it is treated as object or as an interface the value type implements, unless the generic path avoids it. Unboxing casts it back. One allocation is nothing. Boxing inside a tight loop, a logger that formats everything as object, or a non-generic collection is measurable. Generics exist so List<int> does not box each int. LINQ with struct enumerators and Span-based APIs are the runtime pushing the same idea. For a normal CRUD API I care more about chatty SQL than about boxing. I mention boxing when someone stores value types in ArrayList, or when a hot path shows allocations in a trace.",
         "lead": "I ask for a trace before a micro-optimization. If the profiler says the time is in SQL, boxing talk is a distraction. If the profiler says the time is allocations in a parser, then Span, ArrayPool, and fewer boxes are the work."},
        {"t": "qa", "q": "How do you make shared state safe, and why is lock the wrong tool across an await?",
         "a": "Immutable data needs no lock. A request-scoped DbContext is confined to one request, so I do not share it. For a counter, Interlocked is enough. For a cache of independent keys, ConcurrentDictionary is enough. For a critical section that must not run two at a time, I lock on a private object field. The lock statement is reentrant on the same thread and it is released if the block throws. It cannot span an await, because the continuation may run on another thread and Monitor is thread-affine. Across an await I use SemaphoreSlim. A singleton service that touches mutable fields is either immutable, uses a concurrent collection, or serializes access. A static list mutated from requests is a race I expect to see in legacy code.",
         "lead": "I treat a static mutable field in an App Service as a bug the moment we scale to two instances, even if the lock is correct. The lock is per process. The second instance has its own lock and its own memory. Shared state belongs in SQL, Redis, or a queue."},
        {"t": "h2", "text": "Fundamentals they still ask — short answers"},
        {"t": "table", "headers": ["Question", "Answer in one or two sentences"], "rows": [
            ["abstract class vs interface", "An abstract class shares a base implementation and a single inheritance slot. An interface is a contract, and a type can implement many. I start with an interface for a dependency I want to fake. I use an abstract class when subclasses truly share state and behavior."],
            ["virtual, override, new", "virtual allows a derived class to override. override replaces the virtual method. new hides the base method and breaks polymorphism when the variable is the base type. I almost never use new for methods."],
            ["const vs readonly vs static", "const is a compile-time constant baked into callers. readonly is set in the constructor and read at runtime. static belongs to the type. A public const in a library is a versioning trap, because callers embed the value."],
            ["First vs FirstOrDefault vs Single", "First throws if the sequence is empty. FirstOrDefault returns the default. Single throws unless there is exactly one match. On a primary key I use Single or SingleOrDefault so a duplicate fails loudly."],
            ["throw vs throw ex", "throw keeps the stack trace. throw ex resets it at the new throw. I use throw, or I wrap with the original as InnerException when I add context."],
            ["string vs StringBuilder", "A string cannot change. Repeated concatenation in a loop copies. StringBuilder mutates a buffer. For a handful of pieces, interpolation is clear and fine."],
            ["IEnumerable, ICollection, IList", "IEnumerable can be streamed. ICollection adds count and mutation. IList adds indexing. I accept the smallest interface the method needs, and I return a concrete list or a read-only view."],
            ["Covariance", "out on an interface type parameter makes it covariant: IEnumerable<string> can be used as IEnumerable<object>. in is contravariant, used for consumers such as Action<in T>. I mention this when someone asks why a generic interface will not compile."],
            ["Nullable reference types", "string? may be null. string should not. The compiler warns. It does not change the CLR. I enable it so reviews talk about null at build time."],
            ["partial classes", "The compiler merges partial parts. EF migrations and source generators use this. I keep hand-written domain logic out of generated partials so a regeneration does not destroy it."],
        ]},
